{
  "dom_id": "step-q237497",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/8236faf3fbf8835d70f790685b2411a86d7856049f3be7dc1cbf5aceb20b4698.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{5}{7}=5\\,\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $4$ wholes split into sevenths.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/2a391dcb9bf54c47e48058afab64c7d4c95b1c4c3fc9a394e843ca80ace9a9f8.png)\n\n- There are $5$ sevenths in each group, with $3$ remaining.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/c3c87e681f843582842d1db87dee476f21e672b0c749370fe82d5274d2d2f311.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $5$ full groups.\n\n- There are $3$ sevenths left over out of a possible group of $5.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{5}{7}=5\\,\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$",
